/* Shared rules for the eight new-character-creator tabs (Tier 4 #4). Linked by
   every tab document AFTER theme.rcss so these win on equal specificity.

   ── CURSOR ROW LEGIBILITY — the reason this file exists ────────────────────────

   theme.rcss pairs `.item.selected` with an OPAQUE accent fill plus
   `color: {{bg-hard}}`, which assumes the row's text colour is the theme's to set.
   In this creator it is not: every row arrives through `data-rml`, so
   `cata_text_to_rml` has already baked the colour into an inline
   `<span style="color:…">` — and an inline colour beats a class rule.

   Measured on the POINTS tab: an RCSS `color` on the cursor row changed ZERO
   pixels, with and without `!important`, while an RCSS `background-color` applied
   immediately. So the intended dark-on-gold never happens — the semantic colour
   stays and sits on the gold fill at 1.65:1, well under the 4.5:1 legibility
   floor. The cursor row rendered as a blank gold bar on POINTS, SCENARIO and
   TRAITS.

   Dropping the colour in the producer is NOT the fix: on TRAITS it is the only
   channel encoding taken / forbidden / available, and on PROFESSION and SCENARIO
   it encodes affordability. Stripping it would blind the user on the one row they
   are about to press Enter on.

   So mark the cursor with geometry and a DARK fill instead. Every other row
   already sits on a dark ground, so each semantic colour keeps exactly the
   contrast it was designed for — and the cursor row ends up the most readable row
   on screen rather than the least. */

.nc-opt, .nc-scen-row, .nc-prof-row, .nc-row {
    /* Reserved on EVERY row, not just .selected, so the cursor's accent bar does
       not shift row text sideways as the cursor moves. Longhand, not the
       `border-left` shorthand: the shorthand form did not paint here. */
    border-left-width: 3dp;
    border-left-color: transparent;
}

.nc-opt.selected, .nc-scen-row.selected,
.nc-prof-row.selected, .nc-row.selected {
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    border-left-width: 3dp;
    border-left-color: {{yellow}};
    /* Bold as a second, colour-independent cursor cue — the fill step alone is
       easy to miss in the dense three-column TRAITS/BIONICS lists. */
    font-weight: bold;
    /* Overrides theme `.item.selected`'s on-accent (near-black), which would be
       invisible on this dark fill for any row that has NO baked colour. */
    color: {{fg0}};
}

/* ── GROUPING GEOMETRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   The measured complaint is scannability, not spacing: the creator carries 2.5-4x
   the glyph-edge density of the UI_designs references (TRAITS 8.72% vs 1.36-2.55%)
   while its line leading is already comparable (1.41x vs 1.44-1.52x). Adding
   leading would not help; what is missing is anything telling the eye where one
   group ends.

   Only reaches what has an element boundary. Each OVERVIEW pane is a SINGLE div
   fed by one data-rml string with its header baked into the text ("Stats:\n
   Strength: 8\n..."), so a header cannot be given size or weight from here — that
   needs the producer to emit the header as its own element. See
   plans/charcreation-visual-overhaul.md workstream 4. */

/* ── TAB STRIP GLYPHS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The theme's .tab is a plain text item; it now carries an icon + label, so it
   becomes a flex row. Icons are ?proc:runic-icon placeholders (see
   plans/charcreation-visual-overhaul.md for the art hand-off table). */
.nc-tabs .tab {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.nc-tab-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 16dp;
    height: 16dp;
    margin-right: 6dp;
}

.nc-tab-label {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
}

/* ── COLUMN HIERARCHY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   This is the hierarchy the screen was missing: previously every line in a column
   was the same 18dp weight, so a column title competed with its own data and the
   eye had nowhere to land. The title and group headings now have their own
   elements (see nc_desc_pane/nc_desc_row) and can carry weight. */
.nc-pane-head {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 6dp;
    padding-bottom: 3dp;
    border-bottom: 1dp {{bg4}};
}

.nc-pane-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 18dp;
    height: 18dp;
    margin-right: 6dp;
}

.nc-pane-title {
    flex: 1;
    font-weight: bold;
    letter-spacing: 2dp;
    color: {{fg0}};
}

.nc-pane-body {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: block;
    overflow-y: auto;
    /* Bottom gap so an overflowing pane's last row is not sliced flush against the
       pane edge. On a 1366x768-class terminal (170x48 cells) the panes are short
       enough to overflow, and without this the cut lands mid-glyph and reads as a
       rendering fault rather than as "scroll for more". */
    padding-bottom: 6dp;
}

.nc-pane-row {
    display: block;
}

/* Group headings inside a column (Spells:, Wielded:, a skill category). Spaced
   away from the rows above so the grouping is visible without a rule per group. */
.nc-pane-row.heading {
    font-weight: bold;
    margin-top: 8dp;
}

.nc-pane-row.heading:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
}

/* Values sit one luminance step above their labels, so a scan reads the DATA and
   the labels recede. Both are overridden per-row by the producer's baked colour
   where that colour is semantic (trait display colours, red None!). */
.nc-pane-label {
    color: {{fg4}};
}

.nc-pane-value {
    color: {{fg0}};
}

/* ── TRAIT / BIONIC BALANCE SCALE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
   Replaces the "7/12 0/-12" formula, which stated the numbers without conveying
   whether you are leaning on advantages or disadvantages.

   NO ABSOLUTE POSITIONING. The first attempt floated the beam with
   `position: absolute` inside a `position: relative` .nc-scale; RmlUi resolved it
   against .nc-panel instead, putting the beam ~39dp above the pans and straight
   through the top bar. Everything here is flow layout: an arm row
   (pan | beam | pan) with the fulcrum centred beneath it.

   The BEAM rotates (transform from C++); the PANS do not ride it — they are
   siblings nudged by their own `top`, so the numbers stay upright instead of
   inheriting the rotation. `position: relative` on a pan makes that `top` a NUDGE
   from its flex position, so C++ never needs to know the layout's base offsets.
   Beam half-length 140dp and 8deg give an end rise of 140*sin(8) = 19.5dp, which is
   why nc_make_balance() nudges the pans by 20dp — the ends and the pans move
   together instead of visibly disagreeing.

   It sits in its own centred row: wedged into .nc-meta between the points and
   cost lines it read as clutter rather than as an indicator. */
.nc-balance-row {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 2dp 0 8dp 0;
}

.nc-scale {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    width: 340dp;
}

.nc-scale-arm {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    width: 100%;
    /* Tall enough to CONTAIN a pan at full tilt instead of relying on never reaching
       one. nc_scale::max_pan_dp() is 140*sin(8deg) = 19.5dp of travel each way, so a
       ~18dp text row needs 18 + 2*19.5 = 57dp. At 58dp with centred items a pan
       cannot escape into the neighbouring row at any balance value — the full-tilt
       state (one side of the budget spent, the other empty) is hard to reach by hand
       and so is a poor thing to leave to testing. */
    min-height: 58dp;
}

.nc-scale-beam {
    flex: 1;
    height: 2dp;
    margin: 0 6dp;
    background-color: {{yellow}};
    transition: transform 0.25s quadratic-out;
}

.nc-scale-pan {
    position: relative;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    transition: top 0.25s quadratic-out;
}

.nc-scale-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 16dp;
    height: 16dp;
}

.nc-scale-num {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin: 0 4dp;
}

.nc-scale-fulcrum {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 14dp;
    height: 14dp;
}

/* ── WIZARD SHELL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Creation fills the screen. The panel is 98% with a 1% inset (set per-tab, since
   those sheets are linked after this one and win) so the panel's own border reads
   as a deliberate frame instead of running off the display edge.

   Inside it: a step rail across the top, then a row of [prev arrow | stage | next
   arrow], then the exit line. The arrows sit at the far left/right of the PANEL,
   which now IS the screen — the reference art has no visible panel edge, so the
   frame plus the inset is what keeps the composition from floating. */

/* Step rail. Reads as ordered progress rather than as tabs: a continuous hairline
   track with the current step underlined on it, and three brightness tiers for
   done / current / upcoming. */
.nc-steps {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
    border-bottom: 1dp {{bg4}};
}

.nc-step {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 4dp 12dp;
    /* Reserved on every step so the current step's underline does not shift the
       rail's height as you advance. Longhand — the shorthand does not paint. */
    border-bottom-width: 2dp;
    border-bottom-color: transparent;
}

/* Upcoming: present but clearly not yet reached. */
.nc-step {
    color: {{fg4}};
}

/* Completed: brighter than upcoming, dimmer than current, so the rail shows how far
   along you are without competing with where you are. plain fg, not the dimmer fg2 — only fg,
   fg0 and fg4 exist in theme.json, and ui_theme::substitute_tokens turns an unknown
   token into #ff00ffff, i.e. magenta. */
.nc-step.done {
    color: {{fg}};
}

.nc-step.selected {
    color: {{yellow}};
    font-weight: bold;
    letter-spacing: 1dp;
    border-bottom-width: 2dp;
    border-bottom-color: {{yellow}};
}

.nc-step-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 16dp;
    height: 16dp;
    margin-right: 6dp;
}

.nc-step-label {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
}

/* Arrow row: the two navigators flank the working area. */
.nc-shell {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.nc-arrow {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 168dp;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 8dp;
    color: {{fg4}};
}

/* Hover is the click affordance: these are the only mouse targets in the shell. */
.nc-arrow:hover {
    color: {{yellow}};
}

.nc-arrow-glyph {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 44dp;
}

.nc-arrow-key {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-top: 2dp;
}

.nc-arrow-dest {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    text-align: center;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
}

/* Keeps the stage centred when only one arrow is present (first and last step). */
.nc-arrow-spacer {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 168dp;
}

.nc-stage {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 100%;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
}

.nc-exit {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: block;
    text-align: center;
    padding: 6dp;
    color: {{fg4}};
}

/* ── SHARED CHOOSER VOCABULARY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The grouped card tree, its floaty info panel and the sigil legend, used by the SCENARIO
   and PROFESSION steps. Promoted here the moment the second tab adopted the pattern: two
   copies of a visual language diverge, and these two screens are deliberately the same
   shape. Per-tab sheets keep only their own container and status rows.

   The reasoning behind these rules — notch geometry, why centring is safe, why motion is
   transitions and never entry keyframes — lives in plans/charcreation-scenario-tree.md. */
/* ── GROUPED CARD TREE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Replaces the flat list. Two defects the old styling had, both named in review:

   1. The list sat on `.scroll-pane`'s lighter fill, which flattened contrast for every
      row — each semantic colour was designed against the panel's dark ground, and the
      pane's fill removed that separation. The tree container carries NO fill; the cards
      carry their own dark one, so the colours get their intended ground back.

   2. The gold cursor accent butted straight against the row text, reading as a prefix
      character rather than as an edge. The accent keeps its width and the card's content
      is inset from it (see .nc-card.selected below). */
.nc-tree {
    /* Full stage width. The right-hand info pane used to take half of it to display text
       that now lives in a fixed panel under the open category, which is both closer to
       what it describes and cheaper in space.

       Centred vertically: with one group open the content is far shorter than the stage, and
       top-aligning left a large void beneath it.

       `justify-content: center` is safe on the MAIN axis even when the content is taller than
       the stage (all three groups open at a 720p-class window — measured at ~609dp against
       ~558dp available). RmlUi guards its whole main-axis alignment block behind
       `remaining_free_space > 0` (FlexFormattingContext.cpp:550), so on overflow every offset
       stays 0 and the items pack from the start — it degrades to flex-start rather than
       emitting a negative offset that would put the first header out of reach. overflow-y
       then makes the tall case scrollable. */
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    overflow-y: auto;
}

/* Every band takes its natural height and they pack from the top. Letting an open band
   absorb the column's leftover height floated its cards in the middle of the panel,
   visually detached from the header they belong to. */
.nc-band {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    margin-bottom: 6dp;
}

/* Header doubles as a focus stop, so it needs a visible focused state of its own —
   distinct from a card's, since they are different kinds of selection. In the
   all-collapsed opening state these three rows ARE the screen's primary control, so they
   carry menu-row padding rather than list-row padding. */
.nc-band-head {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 8dp 10dp;
    border-bottom: 1dp {{bg4}};
    color: {{fg4}};
    transition: background-color 0.16s quadratic-in-out, color 0.14s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-band-head:hover {
    color: {{fg0}};
}

.nc-band-head.focused {
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    color: {{yellow}};
    font-weight: bold;
}

.nc-band-marker {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 14dp;
}

.nc-band-name {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    letter-spacing: 2dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
}

.nc-band-count {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-left: 8dp;
}

/* Carousel: pager, cards, pager. */
.nc-band-rail {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 6dp 0;
}

/* Always present so the card row does not shift when a page boundary is reached; only
   its colour changes. */
.nc-band-pager {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 18dp;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 20dp;
    color: {{bg3}};
    transition: color 0.16s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-band-pager.on {
    color: {{yellow}};
}

.nc-band-cards {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
}

/* Eight per page. An explicit height, not shrink-to-fit: the row must not change height
   as the cursor moves between a one-line name and a three-line one, and the bands need
   real vertical presence or the whole tree collapses into the top-left corner with the
   stage empty beneath it. */
.nc-card {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 96dp;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    margin-right: 6dp;
    padding: 6dp 7dp;
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    /* Reserved on EVERY card so selecting one does not resize the row. */
    border-left-width: 3dp;
    border-left-color: transparent;
    transition: background-color 0.15s quadratic-in-out,
                border-left-color 0.15s quadratic-in-out,
                border-color 0.15s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-card:hover {
    background-color: {{bg1}};
}

/* Cursor. The 3dp gold edge plus the content inset is the fix for defect 2 above. */
.nc-card.selected {
    border-left-color: {{yellow}};
    background-color: {{bg1}};
}

/* The scenario actually in force — a full outline, so it survives losing the cursor. */
.nc-card.chosen {
    border: 1dp {{yellow}};
    border-left-width: 3dp;
    border-left-color: {{yellow}};
}

.nc-card.unavailable {
    background-color: transparent;
}

/* `word-break: break-word` is what stops a long single-word name being clipped at the
   card edge; wrapping alone cannot break "Wilderness". padding-left is the gap between
   the gold cursor edge and the text — the review complaint, stated as geometry. */
.nc-card-name {
    display: block;
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    padding-left: 5dp;
    word-break: break-word;
}

/* Bottom-anchored: flags left, cost right. */
.nc-card-foot {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding-left: 5dp;
}

.nc-card-pts {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-left: 4dp;
}

/* Flag strip: the storytelling channel. Colour carries valence, so the card reads before
   any single glyph is recognised. */
.nc-card-flags {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    min-height: 14dp;
}

.nc-card-flag {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 14dp;
    height: 14dp;
    margin-right: 3dp;
}


/* ── FLOATY INFO PANEL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Sits inside the open band, beneath its carousel. The panel itself never moves and
   never resizes — only the notch above it travels to the card under the cursor — so
   reading it while stepping through scenarios does not fight the layout. */
.nc-band-info {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
}

/* Mirrors .nc-band-rail: gutter, cells, gutter. Identical flex geometry is what keeps a
   notch under its card at any stage width, with no measurement in C++ or here. */
.nc-info-notches {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    height: 7dp;
}

.nc-info-gutter {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 18dp;
}

.nc-info-cells {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
}

/* One cell per visible card, matching .nc-card's flex and right margin exactly. */
.nc-info-cell {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    margin-right: 6dp;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* A drawn block, not a glyph: the sidebar font has no dependable triangle, and a
   background rectangle cannot fail to render. Reads as a tab joining card to panel. */
.nc-info-notch {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 16dp;
    height: 7dp;
    background-color: transparent;
    /* The notch does not move — one cell per card lights up and the rest go transparent —
       so fading the colour is what makes the cursor appear to glide along the row. */
    transition: background-color 0.18s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-info-cell.on .nc-info-notch {
    background-color: {{yellow}};
}

/* Explicit height: the panel must not grow with a long description, or picking a wordy
   scenario would shove the categories below it down the screen. */
.nc-info-panel {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    /* Room for the three fields plus TWO rows of chips. 164dp fitted only one row, and a
       scenario carrying four vocabulary flags (Really Bad Day: FIRE_START, INFECTED,
       BAD_DAY, CITY_START) silently lost its fourth chip off the bottom of the facts
       column — the card still showed the sigil, so the panel disagreed with it. */
    height: 188dp;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    padding: 8dp 10dp;
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    border-top: 2dp {{yellow}};
}

/* Three columns: art, facts, prose. The facts column carries the hierarchy the old
   single pre-wrapped string could not — small dim labels, bright values, dimmer
   sub-lines — which also removes the blank-line padding that used to separate fields. */
.nc-info-art {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 132dp;
    height: 132dp;
    margin-right: 14dp;
    background-color: {{bg1}};
    border: 1dp {{bg4}};
}

.nc-info-facts {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    overflow-y: auto;
    padding-right: 10dp;
}

.nc-fact {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 7dp;
}

/* Label: deliberately quiet and small. It names the row; it is not the content. */
.nc-fact-label {
    display: block;
    font-size: 11dp;
    letter-spacing: 2dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: {{fg4}};
}

/* Value: the thing being read. Largest and brightest text in the panel. */
.nc-fact-value {
    display: block;
    font-size: 16dp;
    font-weight: bold;
    color: {{fg0}};
}

/* Sub-line: counts and qualifiers, subordinate to the value above them. */
.nc-fact-sub {
    display: block;
    font-size: 11dp;
    color: {{gray}};
}

/* Chips wrap: a scenario with six flags must not push the facts column into scrolling. */
.nc-chips {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 2dp;
}

.nc-chip {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-right: 10dp;
    margin-bottom: 4dp;
    transition: color 0.15s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-chip-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 14dp;
    height: 14dp;
    margin-right: 4dp;
}

.nc-chip-label {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 13dp;
}

/* Prose column: the scenario's own voice, so it gets room and a readable size rather
   than being crammed beside the facts as it was in the old right-hand pane. */
/* Prose column children. The blurb is the thing's own voice and always fits; the detail
   buffer below it is exhaustive and scrolls on its own, so a wordy entry cannot push the
   blurb out of the panel. Tabs with only a blurb simply omit the second element. */
.nc-info-blurb {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    margin-bottom: 6dp;
}

.nc-info-detail {
    display: block;
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    color: {{fg4}};
}

.nc-info-desc {
    flex: 1.1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    overflow-y: auto;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    padding-left: 12dp;
    border-left: 1dp {{bg4}};
    font-size: 14dp;
    color: {{fg}};
}

/* ── SIGIL LEGEND ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Thirteen "sigil :: meaning" pairs spread across the full panel width. Wrapping is
   deliberate: at narrow terminal widths a second row is far better than truncation. */
.nc-legend {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 6dp 8dp 2dp 8dp;
    border-top: 1dp {{bg4}};
}

.nc-legend-item {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-right: 10dp;
    margin-bottom: 3dp;
    transition: color 0.15s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-legend-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 14dp;
    height: 14dp;
}

.nc-legend-sep {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin: 0 4dp;
    color: {{bg4}};
}

.nc-legend-label {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
}


/* Expand/collapse-all control, above the first group. Right-aligned so it sits over the
   carousel's own right edge rather than competing with the group names on the left. */
.nc-tree-head {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    padding: 0 0 4dp 0;
}

/* Reads as a member of the band-header family: same marker vocabulary, same quiet colour,
   same brightening on hover. */
.nc-tree-all {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 3dp 9dp;
    /* A border, because without one this was indistinguishable from the dim captions around
       it and read as a label rather than something to click. */
    border: 1dp {{bg4}};
    color: {{fg4}};
    transition: background-color 0.16s quadratic-in-out, color 0.14s quadratic-in-out,
                border-color 0.16s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-tree-all:hover {
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    border-color: {{yellow}};
    color: {{fg0}};
}

.nc-tree-all-marker {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 12dp;
}

.nc-tree-all-label {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 13dp;
    letter-spacing: 1dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
}

/* ── PIP METER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   One pip per point of a range, drawn as a row of narrow blocks. Shared because SKILLS is the
   second adopter: a stat card's meter and a skill row's level meter are the same idea, so the
   colours belong in one place. Each adopter sizes its own pips in its own sheet — the STATS
   card's meter is taller than a 22dp list row can be.

   Filled pips are TALLER as well as brighter, so a meter still reads in greyscale and for any
   form of colour blindness. Colour is the second channel, never the only one. */
.nc-pip {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 6dp;
    margin-right: 2dp;
    /* bg2, not bg1: the SELECTED card's own fill is bg1, so an unfilled pip on it was exactly
       its background and the empty half of the meter vanished on whichever card had focus. */
    background-color: {{bg2}};
    transition: background-color 0.16s quadratic-in-out, height 0.16s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-pip.on {
    height: 12dp;
    background-color: {{yellow}};
}

/* Three classes, so these beat `.nc-pip.on` above without !important.
   Granted floor: present, but plainly not something the player bought. */
.nc-pip.on.base {
    background-color: {{bg4}};
}

/* Costs two points. Red because that is the colour the stats step already used for
   "Increasing Str further costs 2 points." — now carried by the thing it describes. */
.nc-pip.on.steep {
    background-color: {{red}};
}


/* ── SHARED ROW-LIST VOCABULARY ────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The other half of the creator's language: columns of single-line rows, used by the TRAITS
   and BIONICS steps. Promoted here the moment the second step adopted it, for the same reason
   the card tree was — and under names that do not lie, since a bionics row styled by
   `.nc-trait-row` is a trap for whichever step adopts this next.

   The readability argument is ALIGNMENT: every row is cursor | checkbox | cost | name in
   fixed-width cells, so each cell forms a column the eye can run down. Hierarchy comes from
   brightness and tracking, never from size — the references set everything at one size and
   separate regions with dotted rules. See plans/charcreation-traits-tree.md. */

/* ── DOTTED RULES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   RmlUi has NO `border-style`, and this repo already recorded that a dotted border "rendered as
   nothing here" twice (mainmenu.rcss, mainmenu_theme.rcss). A repeating gradient is the working
   substitute and is what the sidebar HUD uses for its scanlines: 2dp of ink, 3dp of gap. */
.nc-rule {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    height: 1dp;
    margin: 5dp 0;
    decorator: repeating-linear-gradient( 90deg,
        {{bg4}} 0dp, {{bg4}} 2dp, #00000000 2dp, #00000000 5dp );
}

/* The same rule turned 90 degrees, between the columns. */
.nc-vrule {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 1dp;
    margin: 0 12dp;
    decorator: repeating-linear-gradient( 180deg,
        {{bg4}} 0dp, {{bg4}} 2dp, #00000000 2dp, #00000000 5dp );
}

/* Meta bar: pools left, this step's own budget right. */
.nc-meta {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 0 8dp;
}

.nc-meta-pool {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    color: {{fg}};
}

.nc-meta-budget {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
}

/* ── COLUMNS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Equal columns, each scrolling on its own so a long list never pushes the detail panel off the
   screen — the panel's position is fixed for the same reason the card steps' info panel is. */
.nc-cols {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    padding: 4dp 8dp;
}

.nc-colwrap {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
}

/* Column heading: tracked-out caps, brighter on the focused column. Its own dim rule beneath
   ties it to the list without the weight of a full divider. */
.nc-colhead {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 0 4dp 2dp;
    margin-bottom: 4dp;
    border-bottom: 1dp {{bg2}};
}

/* The heading's glyph. Promoted from newcharskills.rcss on its second adopter: it is the same
   glyph the column's own diagram element and the detail panel's art slot draw, so a column, its
   place on that diagram and its description cannot disagree about what is being read. */
.nc-col-sigil {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 14dp;
    height: 14dp;
    margin-right: 6dp;
}

.nc-colname {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    letter-spacing: 3dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
}

.nc-colcount {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-left: 8dp;
    font-size: 12dp;
}

.nc-list {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: block;
    overflow-y: auto;
    /* Bottom gap so the last row is not sliced flush against the pane edge, which reads as a
       rendering fault rather than as "scroll for more". */
    padding-bottom: 6dp;
}

/* ── ROWS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   EVERY row is this height, headings included. That uniformity is load-bearing: C++ scrolls the
   cursor into view as scroll_height / row_count, which is only exact while no row is taller.

   `line-height` above the glyph size is the spacious feel the references have — it sets rows on a
   generous rhythm without the leading being big enough to break the columns apart. */
.nc-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 22dp;
    line-height: 22dp;
    padding-left: 2dp;
    /* NO fill. The markup carries theme.rcss's `.item`, whose {{bg1}} ground turns a column of
       rows into one pale slab that stops dead after the last row — and flattens the contrast of
       every semantic colour in it, since each was designed against the panel's dark ground. That
       is the same defect recorded for `.scroll-pane` at the top of this file, and the same fix:
       the container carries no fill, and only the cursor row and hover paint one. This rule beats
       `.item` because both are single-class and this sheet is linked after the theme. */
    background-color: transparent;
    /* LOAD-BEARING, exactly as .nc-eqp-row records it (newcharprofession.rcss:253): a long name
       wrapping to a second line inside a fixed-height box spills into the row below, so
       consecutive rows draw over each other. It also grows scroll_height, which silently
       desyncs the cursor-scroll arithmetic that assumes every row is this tall. */
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* Hover is the click affordance for a mouse; the cursor row's own fill comes from the top of
   this file so it stays identical to every other list in the creator. */
.nc-row:hover {
    background-color: {{bg1}};
}

/* A sub-heading inside a column. Same height as an item, and NO border: `height` is content-box
   here, so a 1dp rule would make headings 23dp against the items' 22dp and break the uniformity
   the scroll arithmetic depends on. Tracking, caps and the gold the producer bakes in carry the
   hierarchy instead. */
.nc-row.header {
    letter-spacing: 2dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
}

.nc-row.header:hover {
    background-color: transparent;
}

/* Micro-columns. Fixed widths, so cursor / box / cost / name each line up down the whole
   column; this alignment is the entire readability argument for the row layout. */
.nc-cell-cursor {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 10dp;
}

/* The toggle target. Wider than its glyph so it is comfortable to hit, and the only element on
   the row that changes the character. */
.nc-cell-check {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 30dp;
}

.nc-cell-check:hover {
    color: {{yellow}};
}

/* Right-aligned inside its cell, so the brackets stack into a neat gutter of numbers. */
.nc-cell-cost {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 44dp;
    text-align: right;
    margin-right: 8dp;
}

/* nowrap for the same reason the row clips: one long name must not become two lines. */
.nc-cell-name {
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: block;
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
}

/* The +/- steppers. Promoted here from newcharskills.rcss when the OVERVIEW identity rows became
   the second adopter: they flank the value they change, so the mouse gesture is spatially the same
   as the LEFT/RIGHT keys that do it from the keyboard. */
.nc-cell-step {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 14dp;
    text-align: center;
    color: {{fg4}};
}

.nc-cell-step:hover {
    color: {{yellow}};
}

/* Plainly dead rather than absent, so the row does not change shape at a floor or a cap — including
   under the cursor, where the hover colour must not promise that pressing it will do something. */
.nc-cell-step.off {
    color: {{bg3}};
}

.nc-cell-step.off:hover {
    color: {{bg3}};
}

/* ── TALLY LEGEND ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   A vertical icon | name | value list under a diagram: the SKILLS aptitude radar's sector legend
   and the OVERVIEW seal's step digest. Promoted on the second adopter under a name that does not
   lie about what it styles — `.nc-legend*` is already the HORIZONTAL sigil strip on SCENARIO and
   PROFESSION, which is a different thing.

   Scrolls rather than growing: an unbounded legend under a fixed diagram is recoverable, drawing
   over the rule and the detail panel below it is not. */
.nc-tally {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    overflow-y: auto;
}

.nc-tally-row {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    height: 16dp;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.nc-tally-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 12dp;
    height: 12dp;
    margin-right: 6dp;
}

.nc-tally-name {
    display: block;
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* nowrap and clipped, both LOAD-BEARING: the row's height is fixed, so a value long enough to wrap
   inside its own cell draws over the rows beneath it. Measured on the OVERVIEW seal, where a
   scenario name ("One Last Assassination") welded three legend rows into one illegible smudge. */
.nc-tally-val {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-left: 6dp;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* ── DETAIL HEADER AND HINT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ":: NAME" over the shared art | facts | prose panel, and the keys line under it. */
.nc-selname {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: block;
    padding: 0 8dp 4dp 8dp;
    letter-spacing: 2dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
}

.nc-hint {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: block;
    text-align: right;
    padding: 4dp 8dp 0 8dp;
    font-size: 13dp;
    color: {{fg4}};
}